## What to write

Start the page with the line `# Getting Started`.

1. One sentence on what the reader will have at the end of part one, then at most one more.
2. `## Install`: requirements in one sentence, the install command(s) in one code block, one sentence on how to check it worked.
3. One `##` section per step of the first use, in the order a user does them, 2 to 4 sections, each named for what the user does ("Index a repository", "Serve the first page"), never a heading copied from the documents such as "Introduction" or "Overview". For a command-line program: run its main command, look at what it produced, the next command a user reaches for. For a library: the smallest program that uses it, running that program, the next feature a user adds. Use only steps the user documentation shows. Each step: the command in a code block, then at most 3 sentences: what it does and what the user sees when it worked.
